<%!-- ============================================================
  Docs / Host your own pack registry — private pack distribution on
  customer infrastructure, built with the same tool (packctl) that
  publishes the public registry. Distribution ≠ trust, unless a deployment
  configures this registry as its catalog.
============================================================ --%>

<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="pack-registry"
      updated="August 18, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"get-packctl", "1. Get packctl"},
        {"build", "2. Build the registry tree"},
        {"host", "3. Host it anywhere"},
        {"install", "4. Install fleet-wide"},
        {"publish", "5. Publish the next version"},
        {"tradeoffs", "What your registry changes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="pack-registry">
        <:dek>
          An emisar pack registry is a <strong class="text-zinc-200">static file tree over
          HTTPS</strong>
          — host it in a bucket, nginx, MinIO, or an artifact store. <code>packctl</code>
          builds the tree, and each pack installs from an immutable URL pinned to the hash
          you reviewed.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Registry paths become v1 compatibility surfaces. Read
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/compatibility"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Compatibility and deprecation</.link>
        before you publish a stable internal endpoint.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Distribution and trust are separate. The runner checks the supplied <code>sha256</code>
        hash at install and again when it loads the pack, and the control plane auto-trusts
        only entries from its configured catalog. Other packs remain pending until
        an admin trusts their exact version and hash.
      </p>
      <.agent_skill_cta
        class="mt-8"
        skill="author-pack"
        prompt="Author an emisar action pack, publish it to my own pack registry, and install it hash-pinned on a runner. Use the author-pack skill: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AndrewDryga/emisar/main/skills/author-pack/SKILL.md"
      >
        The <code class="text-zinc-300">author-pack</code>
        skill walks your agent through authoring, publishing, and installing from your own registry.
      </.agent_skill_cta>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This page starts from packs you already authored — the format is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/publishing-packs"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Author your own pack</.link>, and every field is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >the Pack reference</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="get-packctl">1. Get packctl</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <code>packctl</code>
        builds and publishes registries. It is deliberately a
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">separate
        binary</strong>
        from <code>emisar</code>, so your fleet hosts never carry publish code or publisher
        credentials — only the workstation or CI job that builds the registry does. It
        shares the runner's pack loader, so published content hashes match
        <code>emisar pack validate</code>
        byte-for-byte.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Build it from the same signed release tag your runners were installed
        from, so the tool that hashes a pack is the one whose loader will
        enforce that hash — an unpinned
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">go install …@latest</code>
        builds whatever the default branch holds that minute.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="shell"
        copy_text="git clone --depth 1 --branch runner-v<version> https://github.com/andrewdryga/emisar.git emisar-src\ncd emisar-src/runner && go build -o ~/.local/bin/packctl ./cmd/packctl"
      >git clone --depth 1 --branch runner-v&lt;version&gt; \\
  https://github.com/andrewdryga/emisar.git emisar-src
cd emisar-src/runner && go build -o ~/.local/bin/packctl ./cmd/packctl</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="build">2. Build the registry tree</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <code>--base-url</code>
        is wherever you will host it. Tarball URLs in the catalog join onto it:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">packctl catalog build --packs ./packs --out ./dist \
  --base-url https://packs.acme.internal</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The output is the whole registry: immutable versioned and content-addressed
        artifacts, plus two mutable pointers:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format>v1/catalog.json                                  latest catalog (mutable pointer)
v1/catalog/&lt;sha256&gt;.json                         immutable catalog snapshot
v1/suggest.json                                  lean suggest index (mutable pointer)
v1/schemas/*.vN.schema.json                      immutable versioned schemas
v1/packs/&lt;id&gt;/&lt;version&gt;/&lt;sha256&gt;/pack.tar.gz     immutable pack tarball</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="host">3. Host it anywhere</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <code>packctl</code> can publish directly to GCS. It protects immutable objects with
        upload preconditions, so existing objects are never overwritten, and it updates the
        two mutable pointers last:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">GOOGLE_OAUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN=$(gcloud auth print-access-token) \
  packctl catalog publish --dir ./dist --bucket acme-pack-registry</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For S3, MinIO, nginx, or another static host, sync the plain file tree with your
        preferred tool. Upload immutable objects first, then the two mutable pointers:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">aws s3 sync ./dist s3://acme-pack-registry \
  --exclude "v1/catalog.json" --exclude "v1/suggest.json"
aws s3 cp ./dist/v1/suggest.json s3://acme-pack-registry/v1/suggest.json
aws s3 cp ./dist/v1/catalog.json s3://acme-pack-registry/v1/catalog.json</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="install">4. Install fleet-wide</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Install the pack from its immutable tarball URL, pinned to the hash from
        <code>catalog.json</code>
        — the runner rejects a different hash. Use the same URL and
        hash on every runner, and update by installing the new version's immutable URL.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">sudo emisar pack install \
  https://packs.acme.internal/v1/packs/billing-tools/0.4.0/&lt;sha256&gt;/pack.tar.gz \
  --hash sha256:&lt;reviewed&gt;</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Trust the exact pack version and hash on the
        <a
          href={~p"/app/packs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Packs</a>
        page. Actions use account trust, not the download host.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="publish">5. Publish the next version</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Always build against the currently-published catalog so history carries forward.
        <code>--previous</code>
        enforces immutability: a pack whose files changed for an
        already-published <code>id@version</code>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">fails the build</strong>
        — bump the version instead. On a critical fix, republish, then
        revoke trust on the old version in
        <a
          href={~p"/app/packs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Packs</a>
        — the control plane refuses new dispatches for it, and the runner separately
        checks the delivered hash against its installed files.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">curl -fsS https://packs.acme.internal/v1/catalog.json -o current.json
packctl catalog build --packs ./packs --out ./dist \
  --base-url https://packs.acme.internal --previous current.json</.docs_code>

      <%!-- What it does / does not change --%>
      <.docs_h2 id="tradeoffs">What your registry changes</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 pl-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">emisar works unchanged.</strong>
          Runners advertise installed packs no matter where they came from, and trust,
          policy, approvals, and audit treat a private pack exactly like a published one.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pack downloads can stay inside your network.</strong>
          Mirror the public registry tree, or build one from your own checkout of the public
          <code>packs/</code>
          source.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Suggestions and the console still work.</strong>
          <code>emisar pack suggest --catalog</code>
          takes your registry's <code>v1/catalog.json</code>
          URL: it matches this host against your packs and prints each one's immutable
          install line, hash and all. The console lists your private packs like any others,
          because a runner advertises every installed pack.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Install by URL, not by name.</strong>
          Name-based commands — <code>pack install redis</code>, <code>pack update</code>,
          <code>pack diff</code>
          — read the public registry's layout, which this tree does not have. Install from
          the immutable tarball URLs in step 4.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">To retire a version, revoke its trust.</strong>
          Revoke it in Packs, and the control plane refuses dispatch for that version.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <%!-- Next steps --%>
      <div class="mt-16 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 border-t border-zinc-900 pt-8 text-sm">
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/publishing-packs"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          Author your own pack
        </.link>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4">
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"} class="text-zinc-400 hover:text-zinc-200">
            Pack reference
          </.link>
          <.link navigate={~p"/packs"} class="text-zinc-400 hover:text-zinc-200">
            Browse the public registry
          </.link>
        </div>
      </div>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
